"""One chronological article, a simple timeline and direct reference pages."""
from pathlib import Path
import html,json,re
from chart_components import goa,uttarakhand,ssr,delhi
R=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1];D=json.loads((R/'assets/research-data.json').read_text());E=html.escape
def cite(id,label='Source',base=''):
 return f'<a class="citation" href="{base}evidence/#{id}">{label}</a>'
def write(path,title,deck,body):
 p=R/path/'index.html' if path else R/'index.html';p.parent.mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True)
 t=p.read_text() if p.exists() else (R/'research/index.html').read_text()
 base='../' if path else ''
 t=re.sub(r'data-base="[^"]*"','data-base="'+base+'"',t)
 main=f'<main id="main" class="container">{body}</main>'
 t=re.sub(r'<main .*?</main>',lambda _:main,t,flags=re.S)
 t=re.sub(r'<title>.*?</title>','<title>'+E(title)+' | The SIR Files</title>',t)
 t=re.sub(r'<meta name="description" content="[^"]*">','<meta name="description" content="'+E(deck,quote=True)+'">',t)
 p.write_text(t)
sections=[
 ('what-is-sir','What is SIR?',
 '<p>A voter list records who is registered to vote in a constituency. Special Intensive Revision, or SIR, is a process for preparing that list afresh. It checks the entries that should remain, adds eligible people who are missing and removes entries that no longer qualify.</p><p>The Election Commission directs the exercise. Booth Level Officers carry out fieldwork. Electoral Registration Officers decide registration questions. A name missing from a draft list is not automatically a final exclusion: claims, notices, hearings and publication come at different stages.</p><p>The basic sequence is field verification, a draft list, claims and objections, decisions and a final list. Later applications and appeals are separate steps. A voter identity card, a submitted application and an entry on the current roll are different things.</p>'+cite('ECI-2026-05','ECI order and procedure')),
 ('before-2025','Before 2025: the law and the older lists',
 '<p>The Representation of the People Act, 1950 sets the legal framework for electoral rolls. The Registration of Electors Rules, 1960 provide the procedures. SIR operates within that framework.</p><p>Older intensive-revision lists are used as historical references. Their dates differ by state, including 2002–06 references in the present exercise. A link to an old list helps trace a record. Its absence alone does not prove that someone is not a citizen or is ineligible today.</p><p>The date of a legal document matters too. The ECI compilation located here is dated July 2025 and includes the 2021 amendments. Later changes and current rule versions still require checking.</p>'+cite('RPA-ECI-2025','Act compilation')+' · '+cite('RER-1960','Registration rules')),
 ('bihar-2025','June–September 2025: SIR begins in Bihar',
 '<p>On 24 June 2025, the ECI ordered SIR, beginning with Bihar. The process brought questions about acceptable documents, citizenship checks, access to hearings and the time available to respond.</p><p>Bihar’s draft roll appeared on 1 August. About 65 lakh entries from the earlier roll were absent from that draft. The final roll was published on 30 September with around 7.42 crore voters.</p><p>Those two figures describe different stages. Additions and further removals took place between draft and final publication. The draft omissions therefore cannot be treated as a one-to-one count of people finally or wrongly excluded.</p><p>The legal challenge continued. The Supreme Court’s final judgment on the Bihar framework came in May 2026, discussed below.</p>'+cite('ECI-PRESS-2025','Bihar final-roll statement')+' · <a href="state-files/bihar/">Bihar report</a>'),
 ('expansion-2025','October 2025–February 2026: the exercise expands',
 '<p>A further multi-jurisdiction phase followed on 27 October 2025. Each jurisdiction had its own timetable. A date or figure from one state should not be assumed to apply elsewhere.</p><p>In January and February 2026, the Supreme Court issued West Bengal-specific directions on discrepancy notices and unresolved cases. Those orders describe the procedures the Court required. They do not, by themselves, establish whether every later decision followed those procedures.</p><p>Goa published its final roll on 21 February. Its signed report permits a precise draft-to-final check:</p><div class="equation">1,084,992 + 12,166 − 3,812 − 35,780 = 1,057,566</div><p>The calculation brings together the draft, accepted additions and two removal categories. It balances. That establishes the accounting, rather than the correctness of every individual eligibility decision.</p>'+cite('WB-SC-JAN','West Bengal January order')+' · '+cite('WB-SC-FEB','February order')+' · '+cite('GOA-FINAL','Goa report')),
 ('may-2026','May 2026: a wider phase and the Supreme Court judgment',
 '<p>On 14 May, the ECI issued directions covering 19 states and Union Territories. The order set state-specific reference years and schedules. It also stated that documents were not to be collected during enumeration. Later directions and revised schedules must be read separately from these original targets.</p><p>On 27 May, the Supreme Court upheld the Bihar SIR framework. Its judgment also set legal bounds on citizenship-related inquiries and the handling of such cases.</p><p>The distinction is important. Upholding the framework does not decide every local notice, every later form or every exclusion. Those actions remain questions of their own facts and applicable law.</p>'+cite('ECI-2026-05','14 May order')+' · '+cite('SC-GOA-COPY','27 May judgment')+' · <a href="legal/">The court and the law</a>'),
 ('july-september-2026','July–September 2026: local schedules and safeguards change',
 '<p>The original national schedules were not the last word. Uttarakhand’s July operational bulletin clarified a later notice window.</p><p>Delhi’s signed 15 July schedule moved the draft target to 17 August and final target to 19 October. A 19 August order then set draft publication on 31 August and a final target of 4 November. These are historical targets, superseded by later changes. The district directory’s September listing date is not the date of the August order.</p><p>Tripura’s later timetable was reported by the public broadcaster, but the signed replacement order has not been obtained here.</p><p>Tripura’s original signed annexure gave a claims window from 21 October to 20 November. The later reported timetable must be read separately from that original window.</p><p>On 26 September, the Commission announced doorstep document collection directions, follow-up steps for left-out voters and an independent-expert review of ECINet. ECINet is the digital system involved in the process. An announced review is not a published audit report.</p>'+cite('UK-DRAFT-JUL14','Uttarakhand bulletin')+' · '+cite('DELHI-JUL15','Delhi July order')+' · '+cite('DELHI-AUG19','Delhi August order')+' · '+cite('TRIPURA-REVISED-AIR','Tripura timetable report')+' · '+cite('ECI-2026-09','September directions')),
 ('october-1-7','1–7 October 2026: a drive to reach people left out',
 '<p>On 1 October, the ECI instructed officials to compare older and current rolls, visit absent electors and help eligible applicants. This was a direction to carry out follow-up work. Its announcement is not a count of completed re-registrations.</p><p>Uttarakhand’s final-publication report, dated 3 October, allows all 13 district equations to be checked. The totals reconcile: 7,133,785 + 129,872 − 104,708 = 7,158,949. These are admitted claims and objections, not a measured count of wrongful exclusions and restorations.</p><p>Goa announced special enrolment camps on 7 October. The result of a camp still needs to be traced from application to decision and then to the published roll.</p>'+cite('ECI-2026-10','1 October direction')+' · '+cite('UK-FINAL-OCT03','Uttarakhand final report')+' · '+cite('GOA-CAMPS','Goa camps')),
 ('october-9','9 October 2026: limits on Form 7 objections',
 '<p>Form 7 is used for objections to inclusion and requests for deletion. The ECI’s October order limits an individual to ten applications during a claims period and five per month during continuous updating. Separate rules apply to party-appointed Booth Level Agents.</p><p>The existence of a bulk objection does not establish that a name was deleted. The next records to examine are the notice, the voter’s response, the ERO’s decision and the resulting roll.</p><p>This distinction is central to the Karnataka material. The local criminal-proceeding developments remain reported evidence until the authenticated orders and original objection records are checked.</p>'+cite('ECI-F7-OCT','Form 7 directions')+' · <a href="form-7/">Form 7 explained</a> · <a href="state-files/karnataka/">Karnataka report</a>'),
 ('october-12-14','12–14 October 2026: deadlines and the Form 6 dispute',
 '<p>Maharashtra’s signed extension set 26 October as the revised claims-and-objections deadline. The actual Gazette issue remains unverified here. Its application bulletin reports intake, not a total of people restored to the roll.</p><p>Meanwhile, Form 6 raised a different problem. The standard form contains a first-time inclusion declaration. Previously registered voters seeking re-inclusion questioned how that wording applied to them. The exact challenged online version and the broader signed Supreme Court record remain separate missing documents.</p><p>In its 14 October order concerning Nilotpal Datta and another petitioner, the Delhi High Court allowed the two applicants to submit Form 6 for processing under law. It rejected the first petitioner’s concern about a false-declaration accusation in the circumstances recorded. The order does not record completed inclusion or settle every issue about the form.</p>'+cite('MH-EXT-OCT12','Maharashtra extension')+' · '+cite('FORM6-STANDARD','Standard Form 6')+' · '+cite('DHC-FORM6-OCT14','Delhi order')),
 ('october-15-17','15–17 October 2026: another revision stage and Delhi follow-up',
 '<p>On 15 October, the ECI scheduled a Special Summary Revision, or SSR, in 17 jurisdictions. The order deferred schedules for 16 others and stated that SIR had not started in three. SSR is a separate stage, rather than a new name for every SIR outcome.</p><p>The order’s scheduled dates are a draft on 12 November, claims through 11 December, disposal by 28 December and final publication on 4 January 2027, with 1 January 2027 as the qualifying date. These are the targets in that order, not a live deadline service.</p><p>For those SSR jurisdictions, paragraph 23(c)(ii) requires recognised parties to receive a hard copy and readable PDF of each polling-station roll after draft and final publication. The PDF is to exclude photographs. This does not promise an unrestricted public CSV or prove that files were delivered.</p><p>The same order requires personal ERO cross-checks where deletions exceed 2% at a polling station or one person is the objector in more than five cases. These are checking triggers, not deletion quotas.</p><p>Further litigation about the Commission’s decision-making was reported in October. A reported notice calling for a response is not a finding that the allegations are proved.</p><p>On 17 October, reporting described Delhi’s re-verification drive covering about 47.5 lakh draft omissions. The CEO’s official note, available as indexed text, also describes BLO follow-up and reports 232,514 Form 6 applications during the claims period and 212,400 before draft publication. Those figures do not establish how many people were registered. The signed local instruction and matched inclusion results remain unavailable.</p>'+cite('ECI-SSR-OCT15','15 October SSR order')+' · '+cite('TOI-NOTICE-OCT15','Court report')+' · '+cite('IE-DELHI-RECHECK-OCT17','Delhi follow-up report')+' · '+cite('DELHI-OCT17','CEO Delhi statement')),
 ('where-things-stand','Where things stand',
 '<p>The published material establishes orders, timetables, court decisions and some reliable checks of official totals. It does not establish how many eligible people nationwide were wrongly excluded.</p><p>To answer an individual question, follow the whole sequence: was the person eligible, what notice was served, what response was received, what decision was made, and did the correct entry appear on the roll? An application count or a software flag does not answer all of those questions.</p><p>The state reports below explain local developments. The forms-and-law guide explains the procedure. The document list lets you read the original material and see the limits of each source.</p>')]
contents='<details class="story-contents"><summary>In this article</summary><nav aria-label="Article contents">'+''.join(f'<a href="#{id}">{E(title)}</a>' for id,title,_ in sections)+'</nav></details>'
body='<header class="article-top"><p class="eyebrow">INDIA / ELECTORAL ROLLS</p><h1>The SIR story,<br>from the beginning.</h1><p class="lede">What Special Intensive Revision means, how it unfolded, and what the records tell us. Explained in order, from the legal background to the latest developments covered here.</p><p class="dateline">Events covered through 17 October 2026</p></header>'+contents+'<article class="prose main-story">'+''.join(f'<section><h2 id="{id}">{E(title)}</h2>{text}{goa() if id=="expansion-2025" else uttarakhand() if id=="october-1-7" else ssr() if id=="october-15-17" else ""}</section>' for id,title,text in sections)+'</article><nav class="further-reading" aria-label="Read more"><a href="history/">The dated timeline →</a><a href="state-files/">State reports →</a><a href="forms/">Forms and law →</a><a href="evidence/">Original documents →</a></nav>'
write('','The SIR story, from the beginning','A chronological explanation of Special Intensive Revision, its procedure, court decisions and state developments.',body)
# A timeline contains events in SIR, not activity on this website.
skip={'Archive reconciles Goa and corrects Tripura report','GitHub publication review'}
plain={'Form 7 limits issued':'The ECI sets individual application limits and scrutiny safeguards for objections.','Delhi High Court closes two-voter Form 6 petition':'The two applicants are permitted to submit Form 6 for processing under law. The order does not record their eventual inclusion.','Delhi re-verification drive reported':'Reports describe follow-up work for draft omissions. Completed registrations have not been established.','Uttarakhand final publication reported':'The state report supplies final totals and district figures. The arithmetic reconciles.','Reported Form 6 clarification':'Court reporting describes no approval or disapproval of the revised form. The signed Supreme Court record is still needed.'}
rows=[[date,title,plain.get(title,text)] for date,title,text in D['chronology'] if title not in skip]
write('history','SIR timeline','The main events in Special Intensive Revision, in date order.', '<header class="article-top"><p class="eyebrow">TIMELINE</p><h1>SIR, in date order.</h1><p class="lede">The main events covered through 17 October 2026. For the explanation connecting them, <a href="../index.html">read the full story</a>.</p></header><article class="prose timeline-list">'+''.join(f'<section class="timeline-item"><time>{E(date)}</time><h2>{E(title)}</h2><p>{E(text)}</p></section>' for date,title,text in rows)+'</article>')
write('forms','SIR: forms, procedure and law','A plain guide to the main forms, officials, legal provisions and court decisions.', '<header class="article-top"><p class="eyebrow">FORMS &amp; LAW</p><h1>How the process works.</h1><p class="lede">The officials, forms and decisions you meet when reading about SIR.</p></header><article class="prose"><h2 id="process">From fieldwork to the final list</h2><p>Booth Level Officers collect and check information. A draft roll is published. People submit claims and objections. The Electoral Registration Officer makes the registration decisions. A final roll follows, with appeals and later updating subject to the applicable rules and election-period restrictions.</p><h2 id="form-6">Form 6: inclusion</h2><p>Form 6 is the application for inclusion. The dispute covered here concerns how its first-time declaration applies to previously registered people returning after SIR. A completed form is an application, not proof that inclusion has occurred.</p><p><a href="../form-6/">Read about the form wording</a> · <a href="../delhi-form-6-order/">Read the Delhi order</a></p><h2 id="form-7">Form 7: objections and deletion requests</h2><p>An objection must be distinguished from the decision that follows it. The October directions set individual application limits and scrutiny safeguards. Party-appointed agents have separate rules.</p><p><a href="../form-7/">Read the Form 7 directions</a></p><h2 id="form-8">Form 8: changes to an existing entry</h2><p>The Delhi petition sought correction and shifting through Form 8 rather than fresh inclusion through Form 6. The route directed in a particular case must be read from its applicable instructions and order.</p><h2 id="law">The legal framework</h2><p>The Representation of the People Act, 1950 and Registration of Electors Rules, 1960 govern the process. The July 2025 ECI Act compilation is newer than the historical file previously linked here, but later changes still need checking.</p><p><a href="../legal/">The law and the Supreme Court judgment</a> · <a href="../statutory-source-update/">The dated Act compilation</a></p><h2 id="courts">What the courts decided</h2><p>The May Supreme Court judgment upheld the Bihar framework with legal limits. West Bengal orders gave state-specific procedural directions. The Delhi October order addressed two applicants. These are different decisions with different scopes.</p><h2 id="ecinet">ECINet and digital checks</h2><p>Software records and discrepancy flags should be traced to the notice, decision and roll change they produced. An announced independent review is different from a published report.</p><p><a href="../ecinet/">ECINet and the announced review</a></p></article>')
cards=[]
for j in sorted(D['jurisdictions'],key=lambda x:x['name']):
 detail=j['depth']!='Reference file';label='Detailed report' if detail else 'Brief note'
 cards.append(f'<a class="state-card" data-state="{E(j["name"].lower(),quote=True)}" data-scope="{"detailed" if detail else "reference"}" href="{j["slug"]}/"><h2>{E(j["name"])}</h2><span>{label} →</span></a>')
write('state-files','SIR state reports','Browse reports and brief notes for all 36 states and Union Territories.', '<header class="article-top"><p class="eyebrow">STATE REPORTS</p><h1>What happened in each state.</h1><p class="lede">Choose a state or Union Territory. Eight have detailed reports. The others have brief notes identifying the available records and what is still unknown.</p></header><div class="filterbar"><label for="state-filter">Find a state</label><input id="state-filter" type="search" placeholder="Type a state name…"><span id="state-count" aria-live="polite">36 jurisdictions</span></div><div class="state-grid">'+''.join(cards)+'</div><p id="state-empty" class="hidden">No matching state. Try another name.</p>')
write('methodology','Understanding SIR figures','The difference between draft omissions, applications, decisions and published registrations.', '<header class="article-top"><p class="eyebrow">SIR BASICS</p><h1>What the figures mean.</h1><p class="lede">Similar-looking numbers describe different steps in the process.</p></header><article class="prose"><h2>Draft omissions</h2><p>A name absent from the draft roll is not automatically a final exclusion. Claims, objections and decisions follow the draft stage.</p><h2>Applications and accepted claims</h2><p>A submitted application records a request. An accepted claim records a decision. The corresponding entry in a published roll or supplement establishes the recorded registration.</p><h2>Software flags and deletions</h2><p>A discrepancy flag identifies a record for checking. It does not, on its own, establish a deletion or a correct eligibility decision.</p><h2>Net changes in roll size</h2><p>The difference between two totals includes additions and removals. It is not a count of people wrongly excluded. Changes must be compared at the same legal stage and with matching dates.</p><h2>Court directions and outcomes</h2><p>A court order establishes what was directed or decided. Whether an official followed it, and what happened to a particular voter, are separate factual questions.</p><p><a href="../forms/">Forms and law</a> · <a href="../evidence/">Original documents</a></p></article>')
# A former internal-review address now contains only the order's state-by-state scope.
write('audit','Revision stages by state','The state groups in the ECI order of 15 October 2026.', '<header class="article-top"><p class="eyebrow">15 OCTOBER 2026</p><h1>The revision stage in each state.</h1><p class="lede">The SSR order places states and Union Territories in three groups. These categories do not establish the correctness of individual SIR decisions.</p></header>'+ssr('../'))
write('statutory-source-update','The Act and its date','The legal text relevant to the electoral-roll process.', '<header class="article-top"><p class="eyebrow">ELECTION LAW</p><h1>The Act and its date.</h1></header><article class="prose"><h2>The ECI compilation</h2><p>The ECI publishes a Representation of the People Act, 1950 compilation dated 22 July 2025. It includes the 2021 amendments, four qualifying dates in section 14(b) and the Aadhaar-related provisions in sections 23(4)–(6). <a href="../evidence/#RPA-ECI-2025">Read the compilation.</a></p><h2>Later changes still matter</h2><p>A dated compilation does not establish the absence of later amendments. The applicable Act, commencement instructions and rule versions must be read together.</p><h2>The declaration question</h2><p>Section 31 concerns a written false statement with the specified knowledge or belief. It does not establish that every previously registered person applying again commits an offence, or settle the wording of a later online form.</p><p><a href="../form-6/">Form 6 and its versions</a></p></article>')
# Older bookmarks go directly to the state or story, never into another route system.
